---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="TV Mounting Cost in Orlando: What Locals Actually Pay (2026)"
  description="Real TV mounting costs in Orlando and Kissimmee. Flat, tilting, and full-motion mounts. Wire concealment, bracket upgrades, and what drives price."
  ogImage="/images/tv-mounting.jpg"
  schema={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
      {
        "@type": "Article",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/tv-mounting-cost-orlando/#article",
        "headline": "TV Mounting Cost in Orlando: What Locals Actually Pay (2026)",
        "description": "Honest TV mounting cost ranges for Orlando and Kissimmee. Flat, tilting, full-motion mounts, wire concealment.",
        "author": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC."
        },
        "publisher": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC.",
          "logo": {
            "@type": "ImageObject",
            "url": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/images/logo.png"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/tv-mounting-cost-orlando/#breadcrumbs",
        "itemListElement": [
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 1,
            "name": "Home",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": "Guides",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 3,
            "name": "TV Mounting Cost",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/tv-mounting-cost-orlando"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }}
>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">TV Mounting Cost</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">TV Mounting Cost in Orlando: What Locals Actually Pay (2026)</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        Real cost ranges for flat, tilting, and full-motion mounts in Central Florida. Wire concealment, bracket upgrades, and what drives price.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            <a href="/services/tv-mounting" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">TV mounting</a> costs in <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a>, <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, and Central Florida depend on mount type, TV size, wall material, and whether you want wires hidden. Here are real ranges from 15+ years mounting TVs in Florida homes.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">TV Mounting Cost by Mount Type</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Flat (Fixed) Mount</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> $100-$175
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            The simplest mount. TV sits flush against the wall with no tilt or movement. Best for eye-level installations where you sit directly in front of the TV. Fastest to install (45-60 minutes). We use these for bedrooms, home offices, and <a href="/guides/kissimmee-vacation-rental-refresh" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">vacation rental</a> properties where speed and clean aesthetics matter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Tilting Mount</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> $125-$200
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            TV tilts down 5-15 degrees to reduce glare or improve viewing angle when mounted higher than eye level. Common for <a href="/guides/fireplace-tv-mounting-orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">above-fireplace installations</a> or bedrooms where you watch TV from bed. Adds 10-15 minutes to installation vs. flat mount. Most <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a> and Orlando homes use tilting mounts in living rooms.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Full-Motion (Articulating) Mount</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> $175-$300
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            TV extends, swivels, and tilts. Best for corner installations, open-floor-plan homes where you watch from multiple angles, or rooms with tricky window glare. Takes longer to install (60-90 minutes) because of articulating arm weight and balance adjustments. Popular in <a href="/areas/st-cloud" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">St. Cloud</a> and newer Central Florida homes with great rooms.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Pull-Down (Mantel) Mount</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> $250-$450
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Lowers TV 12-24 inches when watching, raises it back up when off. Designed for <a href="/guides/fireplace-tv-mounting-orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">fireplace mounting</a> where heat and height are concerns. Costs more because of the mount hardware ($200-$400 retail) and installation complexity. We install 3-5 of these per month in Central Florida.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">TV Mounting Cost by TV Size</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Larger TVs require heavier-duty mounts and longer installation time. Most contractors (including us) charge slightly more for 65"+ TVs:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-none space-y-2 mb-6 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>32"-55" TV:</strong> Standard pricing ($100-$300 depending on mount type)</li>
            <li><strong>60"-75" TV:</strong> Add $25-$50</li>
            <li><strong>80"+ TV:</strong> Add $50-$100 (requires two people to lift safely, heavy-duty mount)</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Wire Concealment Cost</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Basic TV mounting leaves wires visible. Hiding them costs extra, but it's worth it for a clean look.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">In-Wall Power Kit (Code-Compliant)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> $75-$150
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            We cut two openings (one behind TV, one near floor), run a conduit through the wall, and hide all wires inside. Power cord plugs into a recessed outlet behind the TV. HDMI, cable, and other wires run through the same channel. Works on standard drywall walls. Most common wire concealment method in Florida homes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Paintable Cable Raceway</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> $50-$100
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Surface-mounted plastic channel painted to match the wall. Visible if you look closely, but clean and neat. Best for brick, tile, or concrete walls where in-wall routing is hard. Takes 15-20 minutes to install and paint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">New Outlet Installation (Requires Electrician)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Cost:</strong> $200-$500
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If there's no existing outlet near the TV location, an electrician runs a new circuit. Completely hides the power cord. HDMI and other cables still need in-wall routing or raceway.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Code compliance:</strong> In Florida, running extension cords or standard power cords through walls violates electrical code. Always use an in-wall power kit (UL-listed) or hire an electrician to add an outlet. DIY wire concealment with a standard extension cord is a fire hazard and fails inspections.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What Drives TV Mounting Cost?</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">1. Mount Type</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Flat mounts cost less than tilting, tilting costs less than full-motion, full-motion costs less than pull-down. More moving parts = higher mount cost + longer installation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">2. Wall Type</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Drywall with studs:</strong> Standard pricing. Takes 45-60 minutes.</li>
            <li><strong>Concrete or block:</strong> Add $50-$100. Requires masonry anchors and hammer drill.</li>
            <li><strong>Brick or stone veneer:</strong> Add $50-$100. Drilling through tile or stone takes longer.</li>
            <li><strong>Metal studs:</strong> Add $25-$50. Requires toggle bolts or blocking (we carry both).</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">3. Wire Concealment</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            In-wall routing adds 30-45 minutes and $75-$150 to the quote. Paintable raceway is faster and cheaper ($50-$100). Exposed wires cost nothing but look messy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">4. TV Size & Weight</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            65"+ TVs require heavier mounts, two-person lift, and more time to balance on articulating arms. Add $25-$100 depending on size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">5. Soundbar or Component Shelf</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Mounting a soundbar under the TV adds 15-20 minutes and $50-$75. Floating shelf for cable box or gaming console adds $50-$100.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Full TV Mounting Packages (Real Pricing)</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Here's what most Orlando and Kissimmee clients actually pay for complete installations:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-none space-y-2 mb-6 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Basic mount (55" TV, flat mount, exposed wires):</strong> $100-$150</li>
            <li><strong>Standard mount (55" TV, tilting mount, in-wall wires):</strong> $200-$300</li>
            <li><strong>Full package (65" TV, full-motion mount, in-wall wires, soundbar):</strong> $350-$450</li>
            <li><strong>Premium fireplace install (65" TV, pull-down mount, in-wall wires):</strong> $500-$700</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What's Included in the Cost?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Our TV mounting quotes include:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li>Mount bracket (commercial-grade, rated for your TV weight)</li>
            <li>All mounting hardware (lag bolts, washers, anchors)</li>
            <li>Stud finder and level (we find studs, mount level, double-check)</li>
            <li>HDMI cable if needed (included up to 6 feet)</li>
            <li>TV setup and cable connection (we plug everything in and test it)</li>
            <li>Drywall patching if we need to move the mount (rare, but we fix mistakes)</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            If a quote seems too cheap, ask what's included. Some contractors skip the mount bracket (you buy it separately), don't test the TV, or leave drywall holes unpainted.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">DIY vs. Hiring a Pro</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            TV mount kits cost $20-$100 retail. Sounds cheaper than hiring a pro at $150-$300, but:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Missed studs:</strong> Drywall anchors can't hold 65" TVs. TV falls, cracks, maybe injures someone.</li>
            <li><strong>Wrong height:</strong> Most DIYers mount TVs too high. Ideal height is eye level when seated (42-50 inches to TV center).</li>
            <li><strong>Crooked mount:</strong> Off by 2 degrees looks fine until you turn the TV on, then it's obvious.</li>
            <li><strong>Wire mess:</strong> Exposed cables look bad and aren't code-compliant if you run power through walls.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            For a 32" bedroom TV on a simple flat mount, DIY might work. For living room installs, 60"+ TVs, or wire concealment, the $150-$300 cost is worth avoiding the risk.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            TV mounting in Orlando costs $100-$300 for standard installs, $200-$450 with wire concealment, $350-$700 for fireplace or premium setups. Mount type, TV size, wall material, and wire concealment drive price. Get an on-site estimate for accurate pricing—free in Central Florida.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-8 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide">Need TV Mounting in Orlando or Kissimmee?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-6">
              All mount types, all wall types, wire concealment. Free estimates and honest pricing.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary">
                Get Free Estimate
              </a>
              <a href="tel:4072446873" class="btn-secondary">
                Call (407) 244-6873
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-sans">
              <a href="/guides" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">← Back to All Guides</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection
    title="Professional TV Mounting"
    description="Secure mounting, clean wire concealment, perfect height."
  />

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
